# TIL — 2026-03-18: Object.hasOwn for safe property checks

- Use Object.hasOwn(obj, key) instead of obj.hasOwnProperty(key) or Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, key).
- It's shorter, avoids prototype-pollution pitfalls, and is clearer in intent.

```js
// safe property check (ES2022+)
const obj = Object.create(null);
obj.x = 1;

console.log(Object.hasOwn(obj, 'x'));        // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(obj, 'toString')); // false

// typical fallback for older environments:
const hasOwn = (o, k) => Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(o, k);
console.log(hasOwn(obj, 'x')); // true
```

- Useful in loops, validation, and when objects may not inherit from Object.prototype.